\title{HASHPIPE-GAT: Sub-Logarithmic Attention for Billion-Scale Graphs}  
                   ^
Warning 8 in paper.tex line 47: Wrong length of dash may have been used.
Graph attention networks deliver state-of-the-art accuracy on many graph-learning tasks but remain prohibitively expensive on real-world power-law graphs whose hub nodes own up to one million neighbours. Even the most recent efficient kernels still spend $\Theta(\log d)$ work per high-degree node at every epoch and repeatedly recompute almost identical attention patterns. We introduce HASHPIPE-GAT, a fully pipelined accelerator that lowers the expected per-node cost to $O(|C| + \log \log d + T)$ through four ideas: (i) an epoch-wise progressive top-$K$ cache that reuses the neighbours with the largest past attention weights; (ii) a three-stage 16/32/64-bit SimHash cascade that early-rejects edges using warp-level bit counting; (iii) a signed CountSketch estimator that replaces exact query--key dot-products with an $(\epsilon,\delta)$ relative-error approximation; and (iv) layer-shared memoisation combined with $2{:}4$ weight sparsity and activation compression. Implemented as a single fused CUDA kernel in PyTorch, HASHPIPE-GAT attains $1.5$–$4\times$ speed-ups over HiPPO-GAT while matching or exceeding its accuracy on Reddit, OGB-Arxiv, OGB-Products and OGB-Papers100M, and is the first method that trains a 12-layer full-batch model on Papers100M within 30~GB of GPU memory. A micro-study confirms that the empirical sketch error stays beneath the theoretical bound and that $96\%$ of true top-32 edges survive the stochastic filter, while a hub-stress test up to degree~$10^6$ shows a $30\times$ cost reduction after five epochs. These findings establish temporal reuse and probabilistic sketching as practical tools for billion-scale graph attention.  
