Graph Attention Networks (GATs) enrich node representations by weighting incident edges according to learned query--key similarities and have become a standard choice for node classification, link prediction and recommendation. However, their quadratic dependence on neighbourhood size renders na\"{i}ve implementations intractable for heavy-tailed degree distributions that dominate social, citation and web graphs. A single hub with one million neighbours demands more than a billion floating-point operations per layer---well beyond the budget of commodity GPUs.  
